{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Of their carcasses you shall not touch</b> — This is not a warning to prohibit us from touching, but rather it says, \"Of their carcasses you shall not touch, for they are impure to you\" — meaning, you shall not touch them unless you are willing to become impure. The meaning is to say that anyone who touches them should be considered impure and must guard himself from the Sanctuary and from consecrated things. And perhaps the midrash that our Sages stated (Torat Kohanim chapter 4:9), \"Of their carcasses you shall not touch\" — ברגל (on the Festival), means that you shall not touch it at a time when you desire to be pure, for they are impure and you shall not go up to them on the Festival. But touching is not prohibited by a negative commandment, for one who touches a carcass on the Festival is not among those who receive the forty lashes. And what they said (Rosh HaShanah 16b), \"A person is obligated to purify himself on the Festival\" — is a commandment from the words of the Scribes, and there is in this no positive or negative commandment from the Torah, aside from the commandment of the pilgrimage ascent. Or perhaps \"Of their carcasses you shall not touch\" on the Festival is an asmakhta (scriptural support), like many other asmakhtot for which there are variant readings there in Torat Kohanim. And they further taught there (chapter 4:10): Others say: Is it possible that if a person touched a carcass he would receive forty lashes? Scripture says, \"And by these you shall become impure\" (Leviticus 11:24). Is it possible that if a person saw the carcath he should go and become impure? Scripture says, \"Of their carcasses you shall not touch.\" How so? You should say: it is optional (רשות). And this is the essential principle of the law from the Torah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase ברגל appears in the Sages' midrash (Torat Kohanim) and Ramban explores whether it means 'on the Festival' or relates to the act of touching; I rendered it literally as 'on the Festival' where Ramban explicitly glosses it that way, and left the Hebrew in other instances where his argument hinges on the dual reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:39.807662134Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}